    description:
      'A first college writing course that treats prose as a public act: you learn to read a rhetorical situation, make a claim a stranger can disagree with, and support it with evidence you actually understand. You will write summaries that do not steal, paraphrases that do not echo, and a researched argument that cites MLA or APA without cargo-cult formatting. The work is revision, not inspiration. AI may quiz you or line-edit a paragraph you already wrote; it may not write the essay, and every borrowed sentence is cited.',
    whyLanguageOrBook:
      'Purdue OWL for citation mechanics, Open Oregon The Word on College Reading and Writing for process, and Amy Guptill Writing in College for the jump from five-paragraph school writing to a real academic argument. They Say / I Say (Graff and Birkenstein) is the O’Reilly companion because its templates make the conversational move visible without turning you into a formula. We do not use a commercial handbook you have to buy to pass.',
