    gitWorkflow: false,
    description:
      'The research-writing course: you invent a question worth months of attention, find sources a library (or the open web used like a library) can actually supply, and join a conversation instead of collecting quotations. The spine is an 8–10 page research paper on computing and society, built through an annotated bibliography, a literature conversation, a counterargument, and a revision workshop. You will also learn to put a figure in prose without letting the figure do the thinking. Heavy on purpose — this is the writing that later design docs and capstone reports assume you can already do.',
    whyLanguageOrBook:
      'Same free stack as ENG 101 (Guptill, The Word, Purdue OWL), now used for research rather than first-year moves. Booth, Colomb, and Williams The Craft of Research is the O’Reilly spine because it treats a paper as a research problem, not a theme. We keep computing-and-society as the default topic domain so the paper can live in your portfolio beside code.',
    outcomes: [
      'Frame a research question that is narrow enough to answer in 8–10 pages and live enough that sources disagree.',
      'Search library databases and the open web with a documented method, and keep a research log.',
      'Evaluate sources for authority, method, bias, and fit — not for "I agree."',
      'Write an annotated bibliography and a literature conversation that maps agreement and dispute.',
      'Choose a structure (IMRaD-ish or humanities essay) on purpose and outline before drafting.',
      'Steelman a counterargument and revise globally after feedback.',
      'Integrate a table or figure as evidence in prose, with a caption and a claim the image does not get to make alone.',
      'Deliver an 8–10 page research paper and a two-minute oral abstract a busy engineer could follow.',
    ],
